//! An RSA-1024 key pair and the two encrypted blobs a client sends back.
//!
//! Fixed, published test material — **not secrets**. Every byte here was
//! generated by `openssl` on a developer's machine and belongs to nothing.
//! The private key is in the repository on purpose, which is only acceptable
//! because it has never protected anything and never will.
//!
//! # Why a fixture rather than a generated key
//!
//! [`crate::login`] can generate a key pair, encode it, and decrypt something
//! it encrypted itself, and all of that agrees with itself under any DER
//! layout — including one no Java client will parse. A vanilla client feeds
//! the bytes of Encryption Request to `X509EncodedKeySpec`, which accepts
//! exactly one encoding, so what has to be checked is that Dust produces the
//! encoding *someone else* produces.
//!
//! These bytes came from:
//!
//! ```text
//! openssl genrsa -out priv.pem 1024
//! openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -in priv.pem -outform DER -out priv_pkcs8.der
//! openssl rsa -in priv.pem -pubout -outform DER -out pub.der
//! openssl pkeyutl -encrypt -pubin -inkey pub.pem -in secret.bin \
//!     -out secret.enc -pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:pkcs1
//! ```
//!
//! so the DER encoding and the PKCS#1 v1.5 ciphertexts are LibreSSL's, and
//! the tests that use them are comparing this crate against LibreSSL rather
//! than against itself. No JVM is needed, which is why this check runs in CI
//! and the `X509EncodedKeySpec` check in `tests/vanilla_status.rs` does not.

