/// [`SECRET_PLAINTEXT`] encrypted **by the JVM**, after parsing
/// [`PUBLIC_KEY_SPKI_DER`] through `X509EncodedKeySpec` — the exact class a
/// vanilla client hands the Encryption Request's key to.
///
/// This is the strongest offline check in the crate on the login key exchange,
/// because it exercises the one thing that cannot be checked by comparing DER
/// blobs: that Java *accepts* the encoding and can use the key. It was
/// produced once, by hand, with:
///
/// ```text
/// KeyFactory.getInstance("RSA").generatePublic(new X509EncodedKeySpec(der));
/// Cipher.getInstance("RSA/ECB/PKCS1Padding")  // what a client uses
/// ```
///
/// which printed `parsed=RSA format=X.509` and this ciphertext. PKCS#1 v1.5
/// encryption is randomised, so re-running it produces different bytes; these
